    "updated": "2026-10-04",
    "answer": "Health Connect's Medical Records feature lets Android apps write and read medical data as FHIR resources in versions R4 (4.0.1) and R4B (4.3.0), organized into MedicalDataSource containers and returned as MedicalResource objects across twelve medical resource types such as vaccines, medications and laboratory results. Writing needs one permission, android.permission.health.WRITE_MEDICAL_DATA, while reading needs a separate READ_MEDICAL_DATA permission per type. Google marks the APIs @ExperimentalPersonalHealthRecordApi, ships them in Jetpack Health Connect 1.1.0-beta02, which requires compiling against the Android 16 SDK, and says the Play policy for medical records access is still being developed. Check FEATURE_PERSONAL_HEALTH_RECORD before every call, because unsupported devices throw UnsupportedOperationException.",
